      "content": "I am currently coordinating the archival implementation for a significant acquisition of 19th-century manuscripts at a university research institution in Belgium. As we establish our long-term bibliographic control protocols, we are performing a comparative analysis of the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) and the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) systems.\n\nCould you provide a technical evaluation of these two frameworks, specifically focusing on their capacity for maintaining taxonomic granularity in the context of non-standard archival materials? I am particularly interested in how each system addresses the challenges of scalability and the preservation of hierarchical integrity when managing highly specialized, unique historical manuscripts that do not fit neatly into standard monographs.",
